        "title": "Thrombosis and venous thrombi",
        "description": "Asked for the definition, classification, and morphology of thrombosis, then for venous thrombus structure, symptoms, location, formation, and Virchow’s triad (2023/05/17). AI explained that a thrombus forms in a vessel or heart chamber and can obstruct flow or embolize. Site categories: arterial, venous, cardiac, and microvascular. Morphology varies by site and age: color, consistency, shape, and wall attachment; older thrombi may organize into fibrous tissue and develop channels that partly restore flow (recanalization). Venous thrombi are typically red, rich in RBCs and fibrin, and often form in deep leg or pelvic veins. DVT may be silent or cause limb swelling, pain, warmth, and redness; embolization to the lungs can cause shortness of breath and chest pain. Virchow’s triad: stasis (e.g., immobility), endothelial injury (e.g., trauma), and hypercoagulability (e.g., cancer, pregnancy, or factor V Leiden)."
      }
    },
